            <ArticleTitle>Visual loss after coronary artery bypass surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>453-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 70-year-old man underwent coronary artery bypass graft complicated postoperatively by visual loss. The diagnosis was nonarteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy. Possible predisposing factors in this patient were hypotension, anemia, a &quot;disk at risk,&quot; and internal carotid artery stenosis. In the postoperative setting, the erythrocyte sedimentation rate may be elevated, as it was in this case and does not by itself suggest a diagnosis of giant cell arteritis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
